<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ch01-02-VectorsIn3D" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Vectors in 3D</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Everything we did with vectors in the previous section transfers to
      vectors in 3D (almost identically). Hence, we will focus first on
      getting used to three dimensions.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title><m>xyz</m>-Coordinate System</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        The 3-dimensional coordinate system represented by the <m>x</m>,
        <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m> axes is called the <m>xyz</m>-coordinate
        system.
      </p>

      <p>
        Other names for this coordinate system are:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Euclidean space
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              3-dimensional space (3D space)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>xyz</m>-space
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbb{R}^{3}=\{(x, y, z) \mid x, y, z \in
              \mathbb{R}\}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      You can draw a 2D representation of 3D space as follows:
    </p>

    <image source="/images/Note 2.1.png" width="70%">
      <shortdescription>The three dimensional space with the axes x, y, and z.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </note>

  <note>
    <p>
      For consistency, we will always draw the <m>xyz</m>-space following the
      right-hand rule as shown below:
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure>
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.2</caption>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
      <image source="/images/2025_08_01_578ccdf8ba98801b0a3bg-1.jpg">
        <shortdescription>The xyz-coordinate system with axes arranged using the right-hand rule.</shortdescription>
      </image>

      <image source="/images/2025_08_01_578ccdf8ba98801b0a3bg-1-1.jpg">
        <shortdescription>The right-hand rule: a right hand, with the thumb pointing upwards, defining the z axis, while the rest of the fingers move from the x axis to the y axis.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </figure>

  <definition>
    <title>Points in <m>xyz</m>-Space</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        In the <m>xy</m>-plane, we represent points by ordered pairs of the
        form <m>\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)</m>. In <m>xyz</m>-space, we
        represent points by ordered triples of the form
        <m>\left(x_{1}, y_{1}, z_{1}\right)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Given a point <m>\left(x_{1}, y_{1}, z_{1}\right)</m>, we call
        <m>x_{1}</m>, <m>y_{1}</m>, and <m>z_{1}</m> the <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>,
        and <m>z</m> coordinates of the point, respectively.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure>
    <caption>Point <m>(6, 4.5, 8)</m></caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_01_578ccdf8ba98801b0a3bg-2.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>The point (6, 4.5, 8) plotted in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    We have infinitely many planes. The <q>main</q> planes are:
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The <m>xy</m>-plane: Consists of all points with <m>z</m>-coordinate
          equal to zero. Defined by the equation <m>z=0</m>. Contains the
          <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> axes.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <m>xz</m>-plane: Consists of all points with <m>y</m>-coordinate
          equal to zero. Defined by the equation <m>y=0</m>. Contains the
          <m>x</m> and <m>z</m> axes.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <m>yz</m>-plane: Consists of all points with <m>x</m>-coordinate
          equal to zero. Defined by the equation <m>x=0</m>. Contains the
          <m>y</m> and <m>z</m> axes.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <figure>
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.2</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_01_578ccdf8ba98801b0a3bg-3.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>The three coordinate planes xy, xz, and yz shown in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sketch the plane parallel to the <m>xz</m>-plane through
        <m>(1,9,4)</m>. Moreover, write an equation for this plane.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The <m>xz</m>-plane is defined by the equation <m>y=0</m>, and any
        plane parallel to it consists of all points whose <m>y</m>-coordinate
        is equal to one fixed constant. Since our plane must contain the point
        <m>(1,9,4)</m>, that constant is 9, so the plane is defined by the
        equation
        <md>
          y = 9
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        To sketch it, draw the <m>xyz</m>-axes (following the right-hand
        rule), mark the value 9 on the <m>y</m>-axis, and through that mark
        draw a copy of the <m>xz</m>-plane. The plane contains the point
        <m>(1,9,4)</m> and, more generally, every point of the form
        <m>(x, 9, z)</m>.
      </p>

      <image source="/images/Example 2.1.png" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>The plane parallel to the x z-plane through the point (1,9,4).</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Vectors in <m>xyz</m>-Space</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        In the <m>xy</m>-plane, we represent vectors in standard position
        using the notation
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle x_{1}, y_{1}\right\rangle</m>. In
        <m>xyz</m>-space, we represent vectors in standard position using the
        notation
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle x_{1}, y_{1}, z_{1}\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        If a vector is given by its starting point
        <m>P\left(x_{1}, y_{1}, z_{1}\right)</m> and ending point
        <m>Q\left(x_{2}, y_{2}, z_{2}\right)</m>, we can shift it to standard
        position by using the formula
        <md>
          \mathbf{v}=\left\langle x_{2}-x_{1}, y_{2}-y_{1}, z_{2}-z_{1}\right\rangle .
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        A vector in standard position is also called a position vector.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure>
    <caption>Vector <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 2,5,3\rangle</m></caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_01_578ccdf8ba98801b0a3bg-5.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>The vector v = (2,5,3) drawn in standard position in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <remark>
    <title>Vector Operations in <m>xyz</m>-Space</title>

    <p>
      Vector operations in <m>xyz</m>-space work exactly the same as in the
      <m>xy</m>-plane. We do operations coordinate-wise.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>,
      <m>\mathbf{u}=\left\langle u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}\right\rangle</m>, and
      <m>c</m> be a real number (scalar). Then
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}=\left\langle v_{1}+u_{1}, v_{2}+u_{2},
            v_{3}+u_{3}\right\rangle</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{u}=\left\langle v_{1}-u_{1}, v_{2}-u_{2},
            v_{3}-u_{3}\right\rangle</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>c \mathbf{v}=\left\langle c v_{1}, c v_{2}, c
            v_{3}\right\rangle</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Note: Geometrically, everything works the same. Adding/subtracting
      vectors can be done using the Triangle Rule. Scaling a vector changes
      its length and potentially flips the direction of the vector. It is
      harder to draw now, though...
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 3,2,3\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle 1,2,1\rangle</m>, compute
        <m>2|\mathbf{v}-2\mathbf{u}|</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Working from the inside out, we first compute the vector
        <m>\mathbf{v}-2\mathbf{u}</m> coordinate-wise:
        <md>
          \mathbf{v}-2\mathbf{u} = \langle 3,2,3 \rangle - \langle 2,4,2 \rangle = \langle 1,-2,1 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Its magnitude is
        <md>
          |\mathbf{v}-2\mathbf{u}| = \sqrt{1^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1^{2}} = \sqrt{6}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        and hence <m>2|\mathbf{v}-2\mathbf{u}| = 2\sqrt{6}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Distance and Midpoint Formula</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given two points <m>P\left(x_{1}, y_{1}, z_{1}\right)</m> and
        <m>Q\left(x_{2}, y_{2}, z_{2}\right)</m> in <m>xyz</m>-space, we
        denote the line segment connecting <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> by
        <m>PQ</m>. Moreover, we have that
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The distance between the points <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> (the
              length of the line segment <m>PQ</m>) is given by
              <md>
                |P Q|=\sqrt{\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)^{2}+\left(y_{2}-y_{1}\right)^{2}+\left(z_{2}-z_{1}\right)^{2}}
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The midpoint on the line segment <m>PQ</m> is given by
              <md>
                \left(\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}}{2}, \frac{y_{1}+y_{2}}{2}, \frac{z_{1}+z_{2}}{2}\right)
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Note that the distance between <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> is simply
        <m>|\overrightarrow{P Q}|</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure>
    <caption>From Briggs, et al., Calculus Early Transcendentals (3e), Figure 13.33</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_01_578ccdf8ba98801b0a3bg-7.jpg" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Two points P and Q in xyz-space connected by a line segment, illustrating the midpoint formula.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <remark>
    <title>Magnitude</title>

    <p>
      Given a 3D vector
      <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>, then
      <m>|\mathbf{v}|=\sqrt{v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2}+v_{3}^{2}}</m>. As before,
      <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is a unit vector if <m>|\mathbf{v}|=1</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <title>Recall: Unit Vector in the Direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m></title>

    <p>
      When working with a 3D vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, we can't define its
      direction by a single angle anymore (since <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
      contained in infinitely many planes). Instead, we focus on the unit
      vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Given a nonzero vector
      <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>, the
      vector <m>\mathbf{u}=\frac{\mathbf{v}}{|\mathbf{v}|}</m> is a unit
      vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 1,2,1\rangle</m>, find:
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) A unit vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) All vectors of length 3 parallel to <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The magnitude of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
        <m>|\mathbf{v}| = \sqrt{1^{2}+2^{2}+1^{2}} = \sqrt{6}</m>, so a unit
        vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
        <md>
          \mathbf{u} = \frac{\mathbf{v}}{|\mathbf{v}|} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\langle 1,2,1 \rangle = \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{2}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        A vector of length 3 parallel to <m>\mathbf{v}</m> points either in
        the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> or in the direction opposite of
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, so the only two such vectors are
        <md>
          3\mathbf{u} = \left\langle \frac{3}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{6}{\sqrt{6}}, \frac{3}{\sqrt{6}} \right\rangle \quad \text{and} \quad -3\mathbf{u} = \left\langle -\frac{3}{\sqrt{6}}, -\frac{6}{\sqrt{6}}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt{6}} \right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Coordinate Vectors</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        In <m>xyz</m>-space, the following vectors are called coordinate
        vectors:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{i}=\langle 1,0,0\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{j}=\langle 0,1,0\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{k}=\langle 0,0,1\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Given a vector
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m>, we
        can also write <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in the form
        <md>
          \mathbf{v}=v_{1} \mathbf{i}+v_{2} \mathbf{j}+v_{3} \mathbf{k} .
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify that
        <m>\langle -2,3,1\rangle=-2 \mathbf{i}+3 \mathbf{j}+ \mathbf{k}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Writing each scaled coordinate vector in component form and adding
        coordinate-wise:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>-2\mathbf{i}+3\mathbf{j}+\mathbf{k} = -2 \langle 1,0,0\rangle + 3 \langle 0,1,0\rangle + \langle 0,0,1\rangle = \langle -2,0,0 \rangle + \langle 0,3,0 \rangle + \langle 0,0,1 \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>= \langle -2+0+0, 0+3+0, 0+0+1 \rangle = \langle -2,3,1 \rangle</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        as claimed.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
